# M-held-bytes, panel 124 (ratified 2026-09-09) and panel 125's soundness lane.
#
# A lease, `c: cstr @ s.lease()`, is a COPY of the bytes that C may read until
# `end_lease(@c)` frees it. What makes a second release impossible is that the
# release nulls the CELL — so the pointer may live in that one cell and nowhere
# else. A copy of it, whether by `=` or into a second cell, would go on pointing
# at the bytes after the cell was nulled, and a read through C would be a
# use-after-free `check` could never see. Panel 125's compiler seat found the
# cell forms; the coordinator found the `=` form by running it. The copies are
# READ below on purpose: an unread one is `unused_binding` from the resolver,
# which stops the check before the lease sweep is reached, and this case is
# about the sweep.

extern "string.h"
    function strlen(s: cstr) -> u64

function main()
    s = "abc"
    c: cstr @ s.lease()
    g = c  #~ lease_escapes
    h: cstr @ c  #~ lease_escapes
    print(strlen(s: g))
    print(strlen(s: h))
    end_lease(@c)
